The scorer returned B+34 with no explicit dead-stone breakdown. The upper White chain contained 14 stones and six enclosed empty points, or 20 area. If Black owned the other 61 points, 61-20-7 = 34 matches the result. This is a consistency check, not an explicit adjudication report. The lower 14-stone chain remained on the displayed board with three liberties and just one confirmed eye.

Lessons for game 9:

- Apply eye-space checks to the ORIGINAL framework, not only new invasions. A large connected group can lose its second eye region while still having four to six liberties.
- Check the boundary points of potential eyes before tenuki. F2/E2 restricted the bottom early; D2/D1 completed the enclosure. Responding only after those points were occupied was too late to identify a rescue here.
- Do not describe left-side interior space as broad without listing its usable empty regions and enemy entry routes. C2/D3 touched a strong Black chain; C4 alone was private.
- A first-line chase can cost both area and initiative even without an immediate capture. B4/B3/B2/B1 contained A5's run but did not capture it, because A6 remained an outside connection.
- Preserve the checked J8 then J6 connection pattern. Connecting to a group with real interior room can rescue a pair in atari; merely joining two small endangered groups at J6 earlier would have left only {J5,J8}.
- Before a pressure move, check what liberties it adds. F3 added none; the neighboring Black groups had other liberties and connection routes. Several low-liberty enemy chains are not automatically a capture.
- Count secured area and conditional area separately. The upper group had only 20 area, while the lower holding depended on unestablished life. Counting both as territory would hide the true deficit.
- Keep training at 20k unless the user chooses otherwise. Eight losses do not demonstrate winning strength. Seek two-eye safety and a scored win before increasing rank; retain the ultimate 9d target.
